The network is fully operational. All connectivity from host_1_1_1_1 (10.1.1.2/24) to every server in the 10.200.0.0/24 range — dns_server (10.200.0.2), web_server_0 (10.200.0.3), web_server_1 (10.200.0.4), web_server_2 (10.200.0.5), and web_server_3 (10.200.0.6) — was verified by live ping with zero packet loss. The user's self-diagnosis (broken uplink or misconfigured gateway) is incorrect in both respects.

The host's IP address (10.1.1.2/24) and default gateway (10.1.1.1) are correctly configured. The gateway IP 10.1.1.1 is properly assigned to the br0 bridge interface on switch_dist_1_1, which bridges eth1 (toward switch_access_1_1_1) and exposes that address to the host. OSPF adjacency between switch_dist_1_1 and router_core_1 is in Full state, and the 10.1.1.0/24 prefix is correctly advertised into Area 1 and propagated via the ABR (router_core_1) into the Area 0 backbone. The server-side gateway (10.200.0.1 on switch_server_access's br0) and its OSPF advertisement of 10.200.0.0/24 are equally correct. No iptables rules, ACLs, or prefix filters exist anywhere on the path. No OSPF errors or neighbour flaps appear in the FRR logs.

Root cause: there is no fault — the reported symptom does not correspond to any actual misconfiguration or failure in the live lab.

Corrective action: no network change is needed. The helpdesk ticket should be closed as a false alarm. If the user genuinely experienced packet loss, it may have been a transient host-side issue (e.g., a process briefly clearing the route table or a momentary ARP miss) that self-resolved before investigation began.
=== END ===